    "statement": "theorem periodicFrom_gcd (f : ℕ → Bool) (p q N : ℕ)\n    (hpN : PeriodicFrom f p N) (hqN : PeriodicFrom f q N) :\n    PeriodicFrom f (Nat.gcd p q) N := by\n  sorry",
    "reason": "THE MISSING COMBINER, AND THE REASON THE PERIOD WALL IS ABOUT POWERS OF TWO. The board can multiply periods (periodicFrom_mul), move a period to an earlier onset (periodicFrom_trans_period), and divide a period from zero by minimality (minimalPeriod_dvd), but it cannot combine two periods of the same sequence. That is the operation the period wall needs. leftDiagonal_period_le asks for a period at most k+1; leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_pow independently supplies the period 2^k. Their gcd is a power of two and is at most the smaller of the two, so this node is exactly what turns 'diagonal k has some period at most k+1' into 'diagonal k has a POWER-OF-TWO period at most k+1' -- and that upgrade is what makes the periods of the first n diagonals totally ordered by divisibility, hence lets one common period serve them all without an lcm blowing past n. Crystal 56 states the wall's honest form as a claim about the doubling positions k_n, which is a claim about powers of two; nothing on the board currently licenses reading it that way. It is used directly by leftDiagonal_period_le_iff_rowNat_period below and is the only step of that node that is not bookkeeping. Why it is true: periods from N are closed under subtraction, because f (n + (a-b)) = f (n + (a-b) + b) = f (n + a) = f n, and Euclid's algorithm is repeated subtraction; the positivity hypotheses you might expect are not needed, since gcd 0 0 = 0 and period 0 is trivially true.",
