","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":67,"newStart":1,"newLines":91,"lines":[" {"proposals": [","   {","-    "id": "leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_two_of_black",","-    "lean_name": "leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_two_of_black",","-    "statement": "theorem leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_two_of_black (m q N j : ℕ) (hNj : N ≤ j)\n    (h0 : PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal m) q N)\n    (h1 : PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + 1)) q N)\n    (hblack : leftDiagonal (m + 1) (j + 1) = true)\n    (hblack2 : leftDiagonal (m + 2) (j + 1) = true) :\n    PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + 2)) q (j + 1) ∧\n      PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + 3)) q (j + 1) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "ONE INDEX OF ONSET BUYS TWO DIAGONALS, NOT ONE. The board's reset step (leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_of_black) settles diagonal m+2 at the first black cell of diagonal m+1, one diagonal per index; this says that when the cell it settles TO is itself black, diagonal m+3 settles at the very same index for free. Why it is true: the recurrence reads diagonal m+3 as a one-bit machine whose OR-driver is diagonal m+2, and that driver is black at j+1 in both readings — black at j+1 by hypothesis, and black at j+1+q because m+2 is now periodic with period q from j+1 — so m+3's value at j+1 is the negation of diagonal m+1 at j+2, which already repeats. Hence m+3 returns at j+1 and bool_driven_periodicFrom_of_return carries it forward. This is the lemma crystal 62 asks for by name: it prices the early settlings, the levels that settle before their driver's self-loop is cut, which crystal 62 measures as the entire distance between the truth (slope 1.25) and the reset cascade (slope 2.00). Measured this session (explorer/seeder_ladder.mjs, explorer/seeder_front.mjs): a ladder that uses this rule, the reset rule, and crystal 45's white branch and never consults the truth reaches level 5001 at index 8486 — slope 1.697, and its index never exceeds the wall's budget of 2k at any of 5000 levels — where the reset-only ladder runs at slope 1.989 and is refuted outright at k = 119 (crystal 62: C_119 = 237 > 236). The double rule fires 1398 times against 2201 single steps, and the two-rule ladder's worst ratio, 1.873, falls at level 118, the same event that breaks the reset-only ladder. Separately, on the true front at shift 16, every one of the 1872 early advances in the first 4000 levels is zero-cost — the front crosses two levels in one row or none — so this rule, and not a partial saving, is the shape the early settlings actually have.",","-    "disclaims": "Does not prove leftDiagonal_onset_le and does not bear on a prize conjecture — it is a claim about the left edge of the cone, where periodicity is already proved. It is one step rule and not a ladder: the ladder that would use it is deliberately not proposed, because a ladder's hypothesis shape is exactly what killed the previous one (leftDiagonal_onset_le_not_of_black_ladder), and 1.697 is a measured slope over 5000 levels, not a proof that the slope stays below 2. No witness is offered: the hypotheses are periodicity statements, which no finite range expresses, and reading leftDiagonal through evolve costs 3^(j+k), so a naive witness cannot reach index 18 where the first transient lives. The packed-row twin rowNat_return_succ_two carries the witness for the same mechanism instead.",","+    "id": "rowStep_agree_succ_two_iff",","+    "lean_name": "rowStep_agree_succ_two_iff",","+    "statement": "theorem rowStep_agree_succ_two_iff (n x y : ℕ)\n    (h : x % 2 ^ (n + 1) = y % 2 ^ (n + 1)) (hb : x.testBit n = true) :\n    rowStep x % 2 ^ (n + 3) = rowStep y % 2 ^ (n + 3) ↔\n      (x.testBit (n + 1) || x.testBit (n + 2)) = (y.testBit (n + 1) || y.testBit (n + 2)) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "THE FREE LEVEL, CHARACTERISED. After the board's own reductions the onset wall is: the agreement width between two rows reaches k+1 by time 2k. The only growth law the board owns is rowNat_return_succ_iff -- the width rises by one when the control bit is black -- and that law alone cannot reach the wall, by measurement rather than by feel. Measured over the seed's own rows at shift p = 16 (explorer/tessera_ladder.mjs, 5000 consecutive steps, width = the lowest bit where rows t and t+16 disagree): the control bit is black on 2022 of those 5000 steps, so a ladder counting only the +1 law gains width at slope 2.473, while the wall's budget is slope 2 -- over budget, so a reset-only ladder is not merely critical, it fails. The width actually gained is 3796, slope 1.317, because 1353 of the 2022 increments are jumps of two or more. This theorem is exactly when a jump of two happens. Once the control bit is black, bit n+2 of both successors is the negation of (bit n+1 OR bit n+2), so the two rows agree there as soon as those two ORs agree -- and an OR forgets information, so it agrees more often than a bit does (5/8 against 1/2 on uniform bits, measured). That coarsening is the free level a wall proof has to price, and it is Gnomon's 'a node whose loop is intact can still be forced, because its two drivers agree' (crystal 62) written as an iff. It strictly generalises the closed rowNat_return_succ_two, which is the single case where both rows are black at n+1, and unlike that node it holds for arbitrary starts -- which the board's own reduction leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_stepMod_preperiod requires, since it quantifies over every start and not only the seed's rows. Crystal 66's OR-to-XOR filter was run on this proposal before it was written down: replacing the two ORs by XORs makes the statement FALSE, checked in the kernel, so the claim is not blind to the term that is rule 30's whole difficulty.",","+    "disclaims": "Does not prove the onset wall and gives no bound on anything. It says when one level is free, not how often -- and how often is exactly the open part. The slopes 2.473 and 1.317 are measured over 5000 steps at one shift on the seed's own rows and are not proved to persist; the 'black control' denominator here is 'bit W(t)-1 of row t is black', which is a different measurement from crystal 62's cascade and its 0.4993, so the two numbers should not be compared without saying that.",","     "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have h2 : PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + 2)) q (j + 1) :=\n    leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_of_black m q N j hNj h0 h1 hblack\n  refine ⟨h2, ?_⟩\n  refine bool_driven_periodicFrom_of_return\n      (fun i => leftDiagonal (m + 1) (i + 2)) (fun i => leftDiagonal (m + 2) (i + 1))\n      (leftDiagonal (m + 3)) q (j + 1) (j + 1) ?_ ?_ ?_ le_rfl ?_\n  · intro i\n    exact leftDiagonal_recurrence (m + 1) i\n  · intro i hi\n    have h := h1 (i + 2) (by omega)\n    dsimp only\n    rw [show i + q + 2 = i + 2 + q from by omega]\n    exact h\n  · intro i hi\n    have h := h2 (i + 1) (by omega)\n    dsimp only\n    rw [show i + q + 1 = i + 1 + q from by omega]\n    exact h\n  · have hb2 : leftDiagonal (m + 2) (j + 1 + q) = true := by\n      rw [h2 (j + 1) le_rfl]; exact hblack2\n    have e1 := leftDiagonal_recurrence (m + 1) j\n    have e2 := leftDiagonal_recurrence (m + 1) (j + q)\n    have hd : leftDiagonal (m + 1) (j + q + 2) = leftDiagonal (m + 1) (j + 2) := by\n      rw [show j + q + 2 = j + 2 + q from by omega]\n      exact h1 (j + 2) (by omega)\n    rw [show j + 1 + q = j + q + 1 from by omega, e2, e1, hblack2,\n      show j + q + 1 = j + 1 + q from by omega, hb2, hd]\n    simp",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalRecurrence", "Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalPeriodicFromStepOfBlack", "Rule30.Proofs.BoolDrivenPeriodicFromOfReturn", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+      "tactics": "  have tb : ∀ r i : ℕ, (rowStep r).testBit i\n      = xor (decide (2 ≤ i) && r.testBit (i - 2))\n          ((decide (1 ≤ i) && r.testBit (i - 1)) || r.testBit i) := by\n    intro r i\n    have h4 : 4 * r = 2 ^ 2 * r := by norm_num\n    have h2 : 2 * r = 2 ^ 1 * r := by norm_num\n    unfold rowStep\n    rw [h4, h2, Nat