nd then arguing about the next bit, and that is only meaningful if the width cannot shrink, so this is the standing hypothesis of the whole family rather than one step in it. There is direct evidence the board wants it public: the one-step congruence behind it, rowStep_mod_two_pow, has been privately re-declared in three separate closed proof files (RowNatModEqIterate, RowNatReturnSuccIff, LeftDiagonalOnsetLeIffRowNatReturn) and the bit-expansion testBit_rowStep in four, so no proof file can cite another's copy. The honest caveat on that count, per the caution in crystals about duplication: these files sit on the same brief chain and the copies may have been carried forward rather than independently derived, so the count is evidence that the fact is needed everywhere, not that four people each thought of it.",","+    "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  intro n hn\n  rw [leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit k (n + p), leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit k n]\n  have hmod : rowNat (n + k) % 2 ^ (k + 1) = rowNat (n + k + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1) :=\n    rowNat_agree_forward (k + 1) T p (n + k) (by omega) h\n  have hlt : k < k + 1 := Nat.lt_succ_self k\n  have e1 : (rowNat (n + k) % 2 ^ (k + 1)).testBit k = (rowNat (n + k)).testBit k := by\n    rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow]; simp [hlt]\n  have e2 : (rowNat (n + k + p) % 2 ^ (k + 1)).testBit k = (rowNat (n + k + p)).testBit k := by\n    rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow]; simp [hlt]\n  have hbit : (rowNat (n + k)).testBit k = (rowNat (n + k + p)).testBit k := by\n    rw [← e1, ← e2, hmod]\n  rw [show n + p + k = n + k + p from by omega]\n  exact hbit.symm",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.LeftDiagonalEqRowNatTestBit", "Rule30.Proofs.RowNatAgreeForward", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+      "tactics": "  have rs_mod : ∀ r m : ℕ, rowStep r % 2 ^ m = rowStep (r % 2 ^ m) % 2 ^ m := by\n    intro r m\n    have tb : ∀ s i : ℕ, (rowStep s).testBit i\n        = xor (decide (2 ≤ i) && s.testBit (i - 2))\n            ((decide (1 ≤ i) && s.testBit (i - 1)) || s.testBit i) := by\n      intro s i\n      have h4 : 4 * s = 2 ^ 2 * s := by norm_num\n      have h2 : 2 * s = 2 ^ 1 * s := by norm_num\n      unfold rowStep\n      rw [h4, h2, Nat.testBit_xor, Nat.testBit_or, Nat.testBit_two_pow_mul,\n        Nat.testBit_two_pow_mul]\n    apply Nat.eq_of_testBit_eq\n    intro i\n    rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, tb, tb]\n    by_cases hi : i < m\n    · have h1 : i - 1 < m := by omega\n      have h2 : i - 2 < m := by omega\n      simp [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, hi, h1, h2]\n    · simp [hi]\n  induction t generalizing x y with\n  | zero => simpa using h\n  | succ t ih =>\n    rw [Function.iterate_succ_apply, Function.iterate_succ_apply]\n    exact ih _ _ (by rw [rs_mod, h, ← rs_mod])",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+    },","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "(List.range 7).all fun n => (List.range 9).all fun t =>\n  (List.range 60).all fun x => (List.range 60).all fun y =>\n    !(decide (x % 2 ^ n = y % 2 ^ n)) ||\n      decide (rowStep^[t] x % 2 ^ n = rowStep^[t] y % 2 ^ n)",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","+      "range": "n < 7, t < 9, x < 60, y < 60, filtered on the hypothesis. Ran true. Claiming agreement one bit wider than the hypothesis gives returns false."","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "stepMod_preperiod_of_return",","-    "lean_name": "stepMod_preperiod_of_return",","-    "statement": "theorem stepMod_preperiod_of_return (n N p x : ℕ)\n    (h : (stepMod n)^[N + p] x = (stepMod n)^[N] x) :\n    ∀ t ≥ N, (stepMod n)^[t + p] x = (stepMod n)^[t] x := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "THE MISSING ADAPTER BETWEEN THE TWO FORMS THE BOARD ALREADY SPEAKS. Every statement of the onset wall's arithmetic form that a computation or an induction can actually produce is ONE equation: leftDiagonal_onset_le_iff_stepMod_return is one equation per k, stepMod_preperiod_le_of_le_11 is one equation per (k, x), and crystal 61's universal form is one equation per (n, r). The interface that consumes them, leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_stepMod_preperiod, demands a ∀ t ≥ 2k statement instead. This is the two-line bridge: iterating one fixed map from a point that has already returned keeps returning, so one equation at N is the whole tail. Nothing composes those landed theorems today without it, and a prover who reaches the wall with a single return equation in hand currently has to rediscover it before the interface will take it.",","-    "disclaims": "Does not prove leftDiagonal_onset_le and does not bear on a prize conjecture. It is pure iteration algebra about an arbitrary function's orbit — it says nothing whatever about rule 30, and it is the only node in this tier that would be equally true of any map at all.",","+    "id": "stepMod_iterate_eq_rowStep_mod",","+    "lean_name": "stepMod_iterate_eq_rowStep_mod",","+    "statement": "theorem stepMod_iterate_eq_rowStep_mod (n t x : ℕ) :\n    (stepMod n)^[t] (x % 2 ^ n) = rowStep^[t] x % 2 ^ n := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "THE JOINT BETWEEN THE BOARD'S TWO PACKED-ROW VEHICLES. The board now carries the onset wall in two different currencies and cannot convert between them for an arbitrary start. Its reductions are in stepMod -- leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_stepMod_preperiod, stepMod_preperiod_of_odd, stepMod_preperiod_of_return, stepMod_preperiod_le_of_le_11, stepMod_iterate_two_mul -- while every bitwise law about the frontier is naturally about rowStep, because truncating at n hides the very bits n+1 and n+2 that the two laws above read. rowNat_mod_eq_iterate is the only bridge on the board and it converts only the seed's own orbit, so an all-starts argument that proves its step lemmas in rowStep has no way to hand the result to the reduction that consumes it. This is that handover: the truncated orbit of a truncated start is the untruncated orbit truncated at the end. It is also the precise statement that rule 30's row map is a T-function in the sense crystal 60 uses -- information moves only upward through the bits, so the levels form a tower rather than unrelated finite systems -- which is asserted in the docstring of stepMod and proved nowhere.",","     "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  intro t ht\n  obtain ⟨d, rfl⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_add_of_le ht\n  have e1 : (stepMod n)^[N + d + p] x = (stepMod n)^[d] ((stepMod n)^[N + p] x) := by\n    rw [← Function.iterate_add_apply]; congr 1; omega\n  have e2 : (stepMod n)^[N + d] x = (stepMod n)^[d] ((stepMod n)^[N] x) := by\n    rw [← Function.iterate_add_apply]; congr 1; omega\n  rw [e1, e2, h]",","-      "imports": ["Mathlib.Tactic"]","+      "tactics": "  have rs_mod : ∀ r m : ℕ, rowStep r % 2 ^ m = rowStep (r % 2 ^ m) % 2 ^ m := by\n    intro r m\n    have tb : ∀ s i : ℕ, (rowStep s).testBit i\n        = xor (decide (2 ≤ i) && s.testBit (i - 2))\n            ((decide (1 ≤ i) && s.testBit (i - 1)) || s.testBit i) := by\n      intro s i\n      have h4 : 4 * s = 2 ^ 2 * s := by norm_num\n      have h2 : 2 * s = 2 ^ 1 * s := by norm_num\n      unfold rowStep\n      rw [h4, h2, Nat.testBit_xor, Nat.testBit_or, Nat.testBit_two_pow_mul,\n        Nat.testBit_two_pow_mul]\n    apply Nat.eq_of_testBit_eq\n    intro i\n    rw [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, tb, tb]\n    by_cases hi : i < m\n    · have h1 : i - 1 < m := by omega\n      have h2 : i - 2 < m := by omega\n      simp [Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow, hi, h1, h2]\n    · simp [hi]\n  induction t generalizing x with\n  | zero => simp\n  | succ t ih =>\n    rw [Function.iterate_succ_apply, Function.iterate_succ_apply]\n    have hs : stepMod n (x % 2 ^ n) = rowStep x % 2 ^ n := by\n      unfold stepMod; rw [← rs_mod]\n    rw [hs, ih (rowStep x)]",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+    },","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "(List.range 9).all fun n => (List.range 14).all fun t => (List.range 40).all fun x =>\n  decide ((stepMod n)^[t] (x % 2 ^ n) = rowStep^[t] x % 2 ^ n)",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","+      "range": "n < 9, t < 14, x < 40. Ran true. Dropping the truncation of the start returns false."","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "stepMod_preperiod_of_odd",","-    "lean_name": "stepMod_preperiod_of_odd",","-    "statement": "theorem stepMod_preperiod_of_odd (B : ℕ → ℕ) (hmono : ∀ n, B n ≤ B (n + 1))\n    (H : ∀ n x : ℕ, x < 2 ^ n → x % 2 = 1 → ∃ p > 0, ∀ t ≥ B n,\n      (stepMod n)^[t + p] x = (stepMod n)^[t] x) :\n    ∀ n x : ℕ, x < 2 ^ n → ∃ p > 0, ∀ t ≥ B n,\n      (stepMod n)^[t + p] x = (stepMod n)^[t] x := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "HALVES THE STATE SPACE OF THE WALL'S UNIVERSAL FORM, USING THE ONE PIECE OF PROVED STRUCTURE THE ROW MAP HAS. leftDiagonal_onset_le_of_stepMod_preperiod asks for a preperiod bound at every width and EVERY start below 2^n; crystal 61 is that statement, exhaustive to n = 31 and the only form of the wall whose induction is not already refuted per level. This says the even starts are free: an even start is 2s, and stepMod_iterate_two_mul (landed) makes its orbit at width n+1 an exact scaled copy of s's orbit at width n, so ordinary induction on the width disposes of it, and 0 is a fixed point. What is left is the odd starts — which are exactly rule 30's truncated left sides with a black left edge, the set crystal 58 identifies as carrying the whole growth of the attractor, and which include the seed, since rowNat_testBit_zero says every real row is odd. Measured this session (explorer/seeder_tails.mjs, exhaustive over all 2^n starts for n ≤ 20): the reduction is lossless — the worst preperiod over all starts equals the worst over odd starts at every width, and the worst over even starts at width n is exactly the worst over all starts at width n−1, which is this theorem's content read as an equation. The same run reproduces crystal 61's numbers independently (worst ratio 1.381 at n = 19) and confirms crystal 57's staircase from a second direction: the longest cycle at width n is 1, 2, 4 stepping at n = 4 and n = 9, the onset-constant staircase.",","-    "disclaims": "Does not prove leftDiagonal_onset_le and does not bear on a prize conjecture. It removes half the state space and leaves the hard half: the odd starts are where every measured worst case lives, so this is a reduction, not progress on the bound. B is a hypothesis, not a construction — the theorem is silent on whether any B with the wall's slope exists.",","-    "under": "leftDiagonal_onset_le",","+    "id": "periodicFrom_gcd",","+    "lean_name": "periodicFrom_gcd",","+    "statement": "theorem periodicFrom_gcd (f : ℕ → Bool) (p q N : ℕ)\n    (hpN : PeriodicFrom f p N) (hqN : PeriodicFrom f q N) :\n    PeriodicFrom f (Nat.gcd p q) N := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "THE MISSING COMBINER, AND THE REASON THE PERIOD WALL IS ABOUT POWERS OF TWO. The board can multiply periods (periodicFrom_mul), move a period to an earlier onset (periodicFrom_trans_period), and divide a period from zero by minimality (minimalPeriod_dvd), but it cannot combine two periods of the same sequence. That is the operation the period wall needs. leftDiagonal_period_le asks for a period at most k+1; leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_pow independently supplies the period 2^k. Their gcd is a power of two and is at most the smaller of the two, so this node is exactly what turns 'diagonal k has some period at most k+1' into 'diagonal k has a POWER-OF-TWO period at most k+1' -- and that upgrade is what makes the periods of the first n diagonals totally ordered by divisibility, hence lets one common period serve them all without an lcm blowing past n. Crystal 56 states the wall's honest form as a claim about the doubling positions k_n, which is a claim about powers of two; nothing on the board currently licenses reading it that way. It is used directly by leftDiagonal_period_le_iff_rowNat_period below and is the only step of that node that is not bookkeeping. Why it is true: periods from N are closed under subtraction, because f (n + (a-b)) = f (n + (a-b) + b) = f (n + a) = f n, and Euclid's algorithm is repeated subtraction; the positivity hypotheses you might expect are not needed, since gcd 0 0 = 0 and period 0 is trivially true.",","+    "under": "leftDiagonal_period_le",","     "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have hzero : ∀ n m : ℕ, (stepMod n)^[m] 0 = 0 := by\n    intro n m\n    induction m with\n    | zero => rfl\n    | succ m ih => rw [Function.iterate_succ_apply', ih]; simp [stepMod, rowStep]\n  intro n\n  induction n with\n  | zero =>\n    intro x hx\n    have : x = 0 := by simpa using hx\n    subst this\n    exact ⟨1, one_pos, fun t _ => by rw [hzero, hzero]⟩\n  | succ m ih =>\n    intro x hx\n    by_cases hodd : x % 2 = 1\n    · exact H (m + 1) x hx hodd\n    · have hx2 : x = 2 * (x / 2) := by omega\n      have hs : x / 2 < 2 ^ m := by\n        have hp : 2 ^ (m + 1) = 2 * 2 ^ m := by ring\n        omega\n      obtain ⟨p, hp, hall⟩ := ih (x / 2) hs\n      refine ⟨p, hp, fun t ht => ?_⟩\n      have hBm : B m ≤ B (m + 1) := hmono m\n      rw [hx2, stepMod_iterate_two_mul, stepMod_iterate_two_mul, hall t (by omega)]",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.StepModIterateTwoMul", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+      "tactics": "  have hsub : ∀ a b : ℕ, b ≤ a → PeriodicFrom f a N → PeriodicFrom f b N →\n      PeriodicFrom f (a - b) N := by\n    intro a b hba ha hb n hn\n    have h1 : f (n + (a - b) + b) = f (n + (a - b)) := hb _ (by omega)\n    have h2 : n + (a - b) + b = n + a := by omega\n    rw [h2] at h1\n    rw [← h1]\n    exact ha n hn\n  have hmul : ∀ a : ℕ, PeriodicFrom f a N → ∀ m : ℕ, PeriodicFrom f (m * a) N := by\n    intro a ha m\n    induction m with\n    | zero => intro n hn; simp\n    | succ m ihm =>\n      intro n hn\n      have e : n + (m + 1) * a = n + m * a + a := by ring\n      rw [e, ha _ (le_trans hn (Nat.le_add_right _ _)), ihm n hn]\n  have key : ∀ a b : ℕ, PeriodicFrom f a N → PeriodicFrom f b N →\n      PeriodicFrom f (Nat.gcd a b) N := by\n    intro a\n    induction a using Nat.strong_induction_on with\n    | _ a ih =>\n      intro b ha hb\n      rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos a with rfl | hpos\n      · simpa using hb\n      · have hdm : a * (b / a) + b % a = b := Nat.div_add_mod b a\n        have hle : a * (b / a) ≤ b := by omega\n        have hbmod : PeriodicFrom f (b % a) N := by\n          have hstep := hsub b (a * (b / a)) hle hb\n            (by rw [Nat.mul_comm]; exact hmul a ha (b / a))\n          have : b - a * (b / a) = b % a := by omega\n          rwa [this] at hstep\n        rw [Nat.gcd_rec]\n        exact ih (b % a) (Nat.mod_lt _ hpos) a hbmod ha\n  exact key p q hpN hqN",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+    },","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "(List.range 12).all fun a => (List.range 12).all fun b =>\n  !(decide (0 < a ∧ 0 < b)) ||\n    (let f : ℕ → Bool := fun n => decide (n % 6 < 3)\n     !(decide ((List.range 40).all fun n => f (n + a) = f n)) ||\n       !(decide ((List.range 40).all fun n => f (n + b) = f n)) ||\n         decide ((List.range 40).all fun n => f (n + Nat.gcd a b) = f n))",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","+      "range": "One concrete sequence, f n = (n % 6 < 3), all a, b < 12, both periods checked over n < 40. Ran true. The variant claiming a + 1 is a period returns false. This is a weak witness -- it tests one sequence, not the quantifier over f -- and it is offered as a sanity check, not as coverage."","     }","+  },","+  {","+    "id": "leftDiagonal_period_le_iff_rowNat_period",","+    "lean_name": "leftDiagonal_period_le_iff_rowNat_period",","+    "statement": "theorem leftDiagonal_period_le_iff_rowNat_period :\n    (∀ k : ℕ, ∃ p, 0 < p ∧ p ≤ k + 1 ∧ ∃ N, PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal k) p N) ↔\n      (∀ n : ℕ, 0 < n → ∃ p, 0 < p ∧ p ≤ n ∧\n        ∃ T, rowNat T % 2 ^ n = rowNat (T + p) % 2 ^ n) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "THE PERIOD WALL WITH NO AUTOMATON IN IT. This is the one recommendation of the attack document of 2026-09-09 ('Two unexplained numbers from today's kernel checks', section 4) that has not landed: its author names it as what they would seed first, 'the only one of the four that puts something new UNDER a wall', because it moves leftDiagonal_period_le into the same N-only arithmetic frame where leftDiagonal_onset_le now lives. That matters concretely rather than aesthetically. Both walls are now claims about the orbit of a single number under r -> 4r XOR (2r OR r); one script measures both, one induction could attack both, and the whole packed-row toolkit the board landed on 2026-09-10 and the four nodes above become available to the period wall, which currently has none of it. Why the equivalence holds. Backward is one line: leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_of_rowNat_agree_any at k = n-1 turns the congruence into a period p <= n = (n-1)+1 for diagonal n-1. Forward is the work, and it has a route that needs no maximum over onsets. For each j < n take the given period p_j <= j+1 and the power-of-two period 2^j from leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_pow; periodicFrom_gcd makes q_j = gcd(p_j, 2^j) a period, a power of two, and at most p_j <= n. periodicFrom_trans_period then moves every one of them onto the uniform onset 2^j <= 2^n that leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_pow already supplies, so no max is needed. Now take q = 2 ^ Nat.log 2 n, the largest power of two at most n: every q_j is a power of two at most n, hence divides q, so periodicFrom_mul puts all n diagonals on the single period q <= n. Finally leftDiagonal_eq_rowNat_testBit reads those n agreements off as the n low bits of rows T and T+q at T = 2^n + n, and Nat.eq_of_testBit_eq assembles the congruence. Measured: the arithmetic side holds for every n from 1 to 700, and the least power of two that works steps up at n = 1, 4, 9, 30, 401 -- which is exactly the known doubling depths 3, 8, 29, 400 shifted by one, an independent reproduction of NKS p. 871 and of crystal 57's staircase from this statement alone (explorer/tessera_ladder.mjs).",","+    "disclaims": "Proves neither wall and bounds nothing. It is a change of vocabulary: it says the period wall and an arithmetic statement about one integer orbit are the same claim, and both remain open. It carries no witness on purpose -- the left-hand side is a quantifier over all k with two unbounded existentials inside it and is not checkable at any finite range, and leftDiagonal is evolve, which gives out near t = 18. The arithmetic side alone was checked to n = 700 and that is reported above as what it is, evidence about one side of an iff.",","+    "under": "leftDiagonal_period_le"","   }"," ]}"]}],"originalFile":"{"proposals": [
