    "statement": "theorem leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_two_of_black (m q N j : ℕ) (hNj : N ≤ j)\n    (h0 : PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal m) q N)\n    (h1 : PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + 1)) q N)\n    (hblack : leftDiagonal (m + 1) (j + 1) = true)\n    (hblack2 : leftDiagonal (m + 2) (j + 1) = true) :\n    PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + 2)) q (j + 1) ∧\n      PeriodicFrom (leftDiagonal (m + 3)) q (j + 1) := by\n  sorry",
    "reason": "ONE INDEX OF ONSET BUYS TWO DIAGONALS, NOT ONE. The board's reset step (leftDiagonal_periodicFrom_step_of_black) settles diagonal m+2 at the first black cell of diagonal m+1, one diagonal per index; this says that when the cell it settles TO is itself black, diagonal m+3 settles at the very same index for free. Why it is true: the recurrence reads diagonal m+3 as a one-bit machine whose OR-driver is diagonal m+2, and that driver is black at j+1 in both readings — black at j+1 by hypothesis, and black at j+1+q because m+2 is now periodic with period q from j+1 — so m+3's value at j+1 is the negation of diagonal m+1 at j+2, which already repeats. Hence m+3 returns at j+1 and bool_driven_periodicFrom_of_return carries it forward. This is the lemma crystal 62 asks for by name: it prices the early settlings, the levels that settle before their driver's self-loop is cut, which crystal 62 measures as the entire distance between the truth (slope 1.25) and the reset cascade (slope 2.00). Measured this session (explorer/seeder_ladder.mjs, explorer/seeder_front.mjs): a ladder that uses this rule, the reset rule, and crystal 45's white branch and never consults the truth reaches level 5001 at index 8486 — slope 1.697, and its index never exceeds the wall's budget of 2k at any of 5000 levels — where the reset-only ladder runs at slope 1.989 and is refuted outright at k = 119 (crystal 62: C_119 = 237 > 236). The double rule fires 1398 times against 2201 single steps, and the two-rule ladder's worst ratio, 1.873, falls at level 118, the same event that breaks the reset-only ladder. Separately, on the true front at shift 16, every one of the 1872 early advances in the first 4000 levels is zero-cost — the front crosses two levels in one row or none — so this rule, and not a partial saving, is the shape the early settlings actually have.",
    "disclaims": "Does not prove leftDiagonal_onset_le and does not bear on a prize conjecture — it is a claim about the left edge of the cone, where periodicity is already proved. It is one step rule and not a ladder: the ladder that would use it is deliberately not proposed, because a ladder's hypothesis shape is exactly what killed the previous one (leftDiagonal_onset_le_not_of_black_ladder), and 1.697 is a measured slope over 5000 levels, not a proof that the slope stays below 2. No witness is offered: the hypotheses are periodicity statements, which no finite range expresses, and reading leftDiagonal through evolve costs 3^(j+k), so a naive witness cannot reach index 18 where the first transient lives. The packed-row twin rowNat_return_succ_two carries the witness for the same mechanism instead.",
